    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Armenia declared its statehood on 21 September 1991 after the failed August coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s dissolution in December recognised its independence.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a> ended in a Russian-brokered ceasefire in 1994 with Armenian-backed forces holding almost the whole former autonomous oblast and most of seven adjacent districts, which they kept until the six-week war of 2020.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>'s offensive of 19–20 September 2023, after a blockade that starved Artsakh of food and medicine, drove almost its entire Armenian population of about 139,000 into Armenia.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> records that the city was founded in the summer of 782 BCE by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argishti-i">Argishti I</a> of Urartu, making the capital of the republic one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities.</p></aside>
    </section>

