    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement.jpg" alt="Karabakh movement" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artsakh_Movement,_February_13,_1988.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ռուսլան Սարգսյան, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> trace their state to Urartu, founded in 860 BCE, and to the Kingdom of Armenia that in 301 CE became the first state to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as its official religion. From the sixteenth century the homeland was divided between the Ottoman and Persian empires; Eastern Armenia passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> by the treaties of Gulistan (1813) and Turkmenchay (1828), while Western Armenia stayed Ottoman. There the Hamidian massacres of 1894–1896 killed close to 200,000 Armenians, and from the arrests of 24 April 1915 between 1 and 1.5 million were exterminated in the Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, which Turkish authorities deny to this day. The First Republic of Armenia, declared on 28 May 1918, was invaded in 1920 by Turkish nationalist forces and by the 11th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> in early December; the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> then ceded Kars, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a> and Surmalu, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p>Soviet Armenia joined the USSR within the Transcaucasian SFSR in 1922 and became a republic of its own in 1936. Stalin's purges killed leading writers and the Catholicos Khoren I; 500,000 Armenians served in the Red Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and 175,000 died. Under glasnost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh-movement">Karabakh movement</a> brought nearly a million demonstrators to Yerevan in 1988; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1988-armenian-earthquake">Spitak earthquake</a> of 1988 killed more than 25,000 people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> pogrom of January 1990 drove some 200,000 Armenians to Armenia. The republic declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 23 August 1990 and statehood on 21 September 1991.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Soviet Armenia had built a modern industrial sector, chemicals, electronics, machine tools and textiles, supplied to sister republics, with agriculture under 20 percent of output. Independence broke those patterns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>'s railway and air blockade crippled an economy that received 85 percent of its cargo by rail, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> closed its border in 1993, and the shutdown of the Metsamor nuclear plant in 1989 brought an energy crisis; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> fell nearly 60 percent between 1989 and 1993, the dram, introduced in 1993, suffered hyperinflation, and agriculture rose above 30 percent of GDP and 40 percent of employment. Growth resumed after the plant reopened in 1995.</p><p>A liberal investment law of 1994, privatisation from 1997 and more than $1.1 billion in loans from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a>, World Bank and EBRD brought steady growth and negligible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, and Armenia joined the WTO on 5 February 2003. Mines yield <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, zinc, gold and lead, and copper prices drove growth of 7.5 percent in 2017; gas and nuclear fuel come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydroelectricity">hydroelectricity</a> is the main domestic source. The diaspora, some 8 million against 3 million at home, finances much reconstruction, and information technology, jewellery and tourism have grown beside farming. GDP stood at $39.4 billion in 2022, when an influx of Russian citizens lifted growth. The population, 3.6 million at its 1991 peak, fell through emigration to 3,081,100 in 2025, 98.1 percent of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war.jpg" alt="First Nagorno-Karabakh War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karabakhwar01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicholas Babaian, Oleg Litvin, Jalpeyrie, MarshallBagramyan, collage: Gulustan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Armed Forces of Armenia were formed after the Soviet collapse in 1991, with a Ministry of Defence from 1992; the prime minister is commander-in-chief. Active forces number about 81,000 with 32,000 in reserve. Armenia ratified the Treaty on Conventional Armed Forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1992, acceded to the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty in 1993, joined the Collective Security Treaty Organisation and sent troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s KFOR in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> in 2004; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> keeps a base at Gyumri as a deterrent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><p>The first war was fought as the state was born. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levon-ter-petrosyan">Levon Ter-Petrosyan</a> and his defence minister Vazgen Sargsyan led Armenia through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-nagorno-karabakh-war">First Nagorno-Karabakh War</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> Armenian forces won, taking practically the whole former autonomous oblast and most of seven adjacent districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> before the Russian-brokered ceasefire of 1994; an estimated 30,000 people had been killed and over a million displaced. On 27 September 2020 full-scale war returned, and the ceasefire after six weeks was seen by many as Armenia's defeat and capitulation. Azerbaijan then blockaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a> until food and medicine ran short and on 19–20 September 2023 launched the offensive that ended it; the European Parliament called it a violation of the 2020 ceasefire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> scholars warned of ethnic cleansing, and almost the entire population fled. In February 2024 Pashinyan said the CSTO had failed its obligations and froze Armenia's participation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan.jpg" alt="Nikol Pashinyan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan">Nikol Pashinyan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%A4tlemine_Armeenia_peaminister_Pashinyan%27iga_(552477480461).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stenbocki maja, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Armenia is a representative parliamentary democratic republic. The constitution followed a semi-presidential model until April 2018, when a reform reduced the presidency to largely representational functions and strengthened the prime minister, who heads the government. Since 1995 legislative power has rested in the National Assembly, a unicameral parliament of 105 members elected by universal suffrage from the age of 18. The country is divided into ten provinces (marzer), each under a marzpet appointed by the government, and the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>. The Armenian Apostolic Church is recognised as the country's primary religious establishment.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levon-ter-petrosyan">Levon Ter-Petrosyan</a>, elected in October 1991, was the first president. The crisis of the system came in 2018, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serzh-sargsyan">Serzh Sargsyan</a>'s third consecutive term provoked protests led by the parliamentarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan">Nikol Pashinyan</a> of Civil Contract; Sargsyan resigned, parliament elected Armen Sarkissian president in March and Pashinyan prime minister in May. After the defeat of 2020 the March of Dignity protests forced early elections, won by Civil Contract on 20 June 2021. Sarkissian resigned in January 2022 and Vahagn Khachaturyan was elected on 3 March 2022. The Democracy Index scored Armenia 5.63 for 2022, still a 'hybrid regime' but the strongest improvement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; Freedom House rated it 'partly free' in 2019, and in October 2023 Armenia ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Statute of the International Criminal Court.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Armenia's weight in the world lies in its diaspora and its history. Some 8 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> live abroad, the largest communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> (1.1–2.9 million), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> (1–1.5 million) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> (250,000–750,000); they finance public works at home and carry the campaign, now more than thirty years old, for recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1915, commemorated every 24 April. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, though among the first to recognise the republic in 1991, has no diplomatic relations with it, closed the border in 1993 and let the normalisation protocols of 10 October 2009 lapse. With both frontiers under blockade, Armenia has built economic ties, including a gas pipeline, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p>The republic joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 2 March 1992 and belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-europe">Council of Europe</a>, the OSCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a> and the Eurasian Economic Union, which it chose in 2013 over an EU association agreement before signing the Comprehensive and Enhanced Partnership Agreement with the EU on 24 November 2017; it hosted the Francophonie summit in 2018. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> conflict remade populations on both sides: virtually all of Armenia's Azerbaijanis, 2.5 percent of the people in 1989, left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, while Armenian refugees from Azerbaijan arrived. Armenia has won the World Chess Olympiad three times, and the world's largest Yazidi temple was completed at Aknalich in 2019.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/sari-gelin">Sari Gelin<span>date unknown</span></a><a href="/songs/mer-hayrenik">Mer Hayrenik (Our Fatherland)<span>c. 1859</span></a><a href="/songs/dle-yaman">Dle Yaman<span>c. 1900</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic's third decade turned on the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>. The 'velvet revolution' of 2018 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikol-pashinyan">Nikol Pashinyan</a> to power and the best democracy scores in the country's history, but on 27 September 2020 the unresolved conflict exploded into a six-week war whose ceasefire many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> saw as capitulation; the March of Dignity protests followed, and Civil Contract's victory in June 2021 kept Pashinyan in office. On 19–20 September 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, after months of blockading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a>, overran it; by 1 October almost the entire population of about 139,000 had fled to Armenia, and the former ICC prosecutor Luis Moreno Ocampo warned that another Armenian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> could take place.</p><p>The defeat broke the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In February 2024 Pashinyan said the CSTO had not fulfilled its security obligations and called it a threat to national security; in March Russian border guards were expelled from Zvartnots airport, and the government announced it would seek EU candidacy, which parliament endorsed on 12 February 2025. On 8 August 2025 Pashinyan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilham-aliyev">Ilham Aliyev</a> signed a joint declaration at the White House committing to a peace deal. Armenia ranks 69th on the Human Development Index; it still depends on Russian gas and nuclear fuel, the Metsamor plant is to be modernised against EU calls to close it, the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and Azerbaijan stay shut, and a population that peaked at 3.6 million in 1991 has only lately begun to grow again.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Armenia, independent since 21 September 1991, carried the memory of the 1915 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> and the first Christian state into a republic at war from its birth: it won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a> in 1994, lost it in 2020 and 2023, overthrew its entrenched rulers in the 'velvet revolution' of 2018, and now turns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and a peace deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</em></p>

